\begin{abstract}
The First Atoms Engine (FAE) is a theoretical propulsion architecture based on
engineered atomic energy-density gradients. Unlike classical thrust systems,
FAE models motion as drift through stability-biased atomic fields generated by
external astrophysical inputs and internal fusion anchors. This paper presents
the mathematical formulation, simulation kernel, and effective motion law of
FAE, and evaluates its behavior under relativistic constraints.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Atomic-scale field manipulation offers a conceptual alternative to
reaction-based propulsion. The First Atoms Engine (FAE) proposes a system in
which motion arises from the interaction between an internal atomic anchor and
an externally defined stability field. The resulting gradient produces drift
without exhaust or spacetime deformation. This work formalizes the FAE
architecture and provides a simulation-based analysis of its behavior.

\section{Atomic Stability Fields}
FAE constructs two atomic stability fields:
\begin{equation}
    \Phi_{A,ext}(t,\mathbf{x}) = f\big(\mathcal{C}(t),\mathcal{S}(t,\lambda),Z_{env},\mathbf{x}\big)
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \Phi_{A,int}(t,\mathbf{x}) = g\big(\mathbf{F}_{Pt/U}(t),\mathbf{M}_{conf}(t),\Psi_{core}(t),\mathbf{x}\big)
\end{equation}
The atomic stability gradient is:
\begin{equation}
    \nabla \Phi_{A}(t,\mathbf{x}) = \Phi_{A,ext}(t,\mathbf{x}) - \Phi_{A,int}(t,\mathbf{x})
\end{equation}

\section{Entropy Reinforcement}
Fusion energy spikes contribute to atomic-scale entropy:
\begin{equation}
    S_{A}(t) = k_{S} \cdot E_{spike}(t)
\end{equation}
The reinforced gradient is:
\begin{equation}
    \nabla \Phi_{A}^{*}(t,\mathbf{x}) =
    \nabla \Phi_{A}(t,\mathbf{x}) \cdot \big(1 + \alpha_{S} S_{A}(t)\big)
\end{equation}

\section{Motion Law}
The effective drift velocity is:
\begin{equation}
    v_{eff}(t,\mathbf{x}) =
    k_{A} \cdot \left|\nabla \Phi_{A}^{*}(t,\mathbf{x})\right| \cdot S_{A}(t)
\end{equation}
with relativistic constraint:
\begin{equation}
    v_{eff}(t,\mathbf{x}) < c
\end{equation}
Acceleration is:
\begin{equation}
    a_{eff}(t,\mathbf{x}) =
    \frac{\partial v_{eff}(t,\mathbf{x})}{\partial t}
\end{equation}

\section{Simulation Kernel}
The FAE kernel evolves five state sets:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\text{EnvState}(t)$
    \item $\text{AnchorState}(t)$
    \item $\text{FieldState}(t)$
    \item $\text{EntropyState}(t)$
    \item $\text{DriftState}(t)$
\end{itemize}

At each timestep:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Construct $\Phi_{A,ext}$ and $\Phi_{A,int}$
    \item Solve $\nabla \Phi_{A}$
    \item Compute $S_{A}(t)$
    \item Reinforce gradient $\nabla \Phi_{A}^{*}$
    \item Evaluate $v_{eff}$ and $a_{eff}$
\end{enumerate}

\section{Simulation Results}
A conceptual simulation over $10{,}000$ seconds shows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Early micro-drift (Bands I--II)
    \item Mid-phase acceleration with increasing entropy (Band III)
    \item Late-phase relativistic drift approaching a fraction of $c$ (Band IV)
\end{itemize}
Acceleration tapers as $v_{eff}$ approaches its relativistic ceiling.

\section{Discussion}
FAE provides a structured model for atomic-gradient propulsion. While the
required field manipulation exceeds current technological capability, the
framework offers a coherent mathematical basis for future theoretical
investigation. The relativistic constraint ensures physical consistency while
allowing continuous acceleration.

\section{Conclusion}
The First Atoms Engine defines a conceptual propulsion regime based on
atomic-gradient drift. Its simulation kernel, motion law, and field dynamics
form a unified theoretical architecture. This academic formulation establishes
FAE as a candidate for future research in atomic-state propulsion.

